WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.660
In an adult mouse, a patch of cortex near the middle maps the whiskers.

2
00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.000
A patch near the back maps what the eyes see.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.100
In the embryo, the sheet that will hold both looks the same from front to back.

4
00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.260
It shows no borders at all.

5
00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.980
So where do the areas come from?

6
00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.320
The process is called arealization.

7
00:00:27.233 --> 00:00:30.413
Cells in the sheet do differ by position.

8
00:00:31.493 --> 00:00:34.673
Progenitors are the cells that divide to make neurons.

9
00:00:35.513 --> 00:00:40.673
They hold transcription factors, proteins that switch genes on or off.

10
00:00:41.753 --> 00:00:45.173
In the mouse, two of them form opposing gradients.

11
00:00:46.233 --> 00:00:48.473
Emx2 is highest at the back.

12
00:00:49.393 --> 00:00:51.253
Pax6 is highest at the front.

13
00:00:52.433 --> 00:00:55.913
Each progenitor reads its position from the mix it holds.

14
00:00:58.067 --> 00:00:59.667
Does the mix matter?

15
00:01:00.687 --> 00:01:02.567
Remove one factor and look.

16
00:01:03.507 --> 00:01:06.507
The areas are read from marker genes at birth.

17
00:01:07.487 --> 00:01:13.807
Without Emx2, the front areas expanded toward the back, and the visual area shrank.

18
00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:18.207
Without working Pax6, the shift went the other way.

19
00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:24.687
Raise Emx2, keeping its slope, and the map moves the opposite way from the knockout.

20
00:01:24.687 --> 00:01:32.107
A factor whose loss and excess move a border in opposite directions is allocating territory.

21
00:01:32.617 --> 00:01:35.637
The gradients need a source of position.

22
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:40.357
At the front, a signal called FGF8 is released.

23
00:01:41.257 --> 00:01:43.377
Think of it as a lamp at the front of a room.

24
00:01:44.497 --> 00:01:48.197
Cells read how bright it is to judge how far forward they sit.

25
00:01:49.037 --> 00:01:54.317
A landmark shows the effect: the barrel field, one cluster of cells for each

26
00:01:54.317 --> 00:02:00.157
whisker. Add a second source at the back, and a partial second barrel field forms

27
00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:06.577
behind the first. Block the signal at the front, and the barrel field moves toward the front.

28
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:11.660
Genes sketch the map, but input shapes much of it.

29
00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:16.580
Thalamic axons bring signals from the eyes, ears and skin.

30
00:02:17.540 --> 00:02:22.620
In rats, fetal visual cortex was grafted into somatosensory cortex.

31
00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.880
Whisker axons grew into the graft, and the graft formed barrels.

32
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.460
Yet in mice where thalamic axons never reach the cortex, area markers still had

33
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:35.960
their normal borders at birth.

34
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:38.900
The sketch forms without input.

35
00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:42.310
So the map is drawn twice.

36
00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:45.350
First comes a sketch from the sheet.

37
00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.190
Gradients and a signal from the edge set where the borders fall.

38
00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.030
Then comes a refined copy, shaped by the thalamic input.

39
00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.330
Move the signal, and the borders move.

40
00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:04.890
Keep this rule: position sketches the map, and input then refines it.
